---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **파도가 지나간 자리.** (`/wave-and-floor`)
 *
 * ── 왜 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *   91편 뒤에 갈래를 열다가 「Screen」 묶음이 **평균 +88.7% · 중앙값 −29.1%** 인 것을 봤다.
 *   문서 하나가 끌고 있었다. 그 하나를 열어 보니 파도였다 — 오징어게임 문서가 앞바닥의 35배.
 *   ⭐ 그래서 물었다. **파도가 지나간 뒤, 바닥은 전보다 높은가.**
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **평균을 앞세우지 않는다.** 중앙값을 먼저 놓는다 — 다섯 편에서 하나가 튀면 평균이 끌린다.
 * ⛔ **못 잰 열다섯 편을 감추지 않는다.** 까닭까지 표로 낸다. 그것이 이 자료의 성질이다.
 * ⛔ **오징어게임을 「바닥이 올랐다」로 안 쓴다.** 그 뒤바닥에 시즌 3 이 들어앉아 있었다.
 * ⛔ **봉우리가 몰린 것을 숨기지 않는다.** 다섯 봉우리가 세 덩어리다 —
 *    그러면 우리가 잰 것이 작품이 아니라 그 몇 달일 수 있다. 그 말을 지면에 그대로 싣는다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않는다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 *
 * 🔴 Astro 앞말(`---` 사이)은 **TypeScript 다.** JSX 를 넣으면 빌드가 죽는다.
 * 🔴 앞말에서 `export function` 을 쓰지 않는다 — Astro 가 딴 스코프로 옮긴다.
 * 🔴 JSX 는 끝 공백을 버린다. 붙여 써야 할 곳은 `{' '}` 를 넣는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import KcwCardnews from '../../components/KcwCardnews.astro';
import KcwShorts from '../../components/KcwShorts.astro';
import d from '../../data/wikitip-wave-floor.json';

const 답 = d.answer;
const 몫 = (v: number) => `${v > 0 ? '+' : '−'}${Math.abs(v).toFixed(1)}%`;

/* 못 잰 까닭을 묶어 센다 — 열다섯 줄을 그대로 늘어놓으면 안 읽힌다 */
const 까닭별 = [...new Set(d.titlesNotMeasured.map((t) => t.why))]
  .map((why) => ({ why, titles: d.titlesNotMeasured.filter((t) => t.why === why).map((t) => t.title) }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.titles.length - a.titles.length);

const TITLE = 'A Korean series fills the encyclopaedia, then empties it. The floor it leaves is a little lower than before';
const DESC =
  `Across four Southeast Asian Wikipedias, ${답.measured} Korean titles could be measured before `
  + `and after their peak month. The median peak was ${답.peakOverFloorMedian} times the floor `
  + `before it, and the median floor afterwards was ${몫(답.floorChangeMedianPc)}.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: d.window,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/wave-and-floor">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">{d.question}</p>
  <p class="q">
    Of twenty titles we fetched, {답.measured} could be measured on both sides of their peak. Their
    median peak was {답.peakOverFloorMedian} times the floor before it. Their median floor
    afterwards was {몫(답.floorChangeMedianPc)} — {답.floorsThatRose} of the {답.measured} ended
    higher than they started.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Five titles, before and after</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Reads are per million reads of that Wikipedia, summed across
      {' '}{d.editions.map((e) => d.editionNames[e]).join(', ')}. The floor is the six months
      before the peak and the six after it, skipping two months either side because those are
      still the wave. Window — {d.window}.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Title</th><th>Peak</th><th>Peak ÷ floor</th><th>Floor before</th>
            <th>Floor after</th><th>Change</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.titlesMeasured.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{t.title}</td>
              <td class="fine">{t.peakMonth}</td>
              <td>{t.peakOverFloor}×</td>
              <td>{t.beforeFloor}</td>
              <td>{t.afterFloor}</td>
              <td>{몫(t.floorChangePc)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The waves are real and mostly modest — a median of {답.peakOverFloorMedian} times the
      surrounding months. What happens after them is smaller still, and points down rather than up.
      We are reporting the median rather than the mean because with five titles one of them moves
      the mean on its own; the mean here is {몫(답.floorChangeMeanPc)}.
    </p>
  </section>

  {답.biggestWave && !답.biggestWave.reportable && (
    <section>
      <h2>The biggest wave is the one we cannot use</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>{답.biggestWave.title}</strong> peaked in {답.biggestWave.peakMonth} at
        {' '}{답.biggestWave.peakOverFloor} times the floor before it — far larger than anything
        else here, and the reason this question was worth asking. Its floor afterwards looked like
        it had risen enormously. Then we read the months that floor was made of.
      </p>
      {d.titlesNotMeasured.filter((t) => t.secondWaveMonths).map((t) => (
        <p>
          {t.secondWaveMonths.map((m) => m.month).join(', ')} are each more than three times the
          floor before the peak. That is not a floor settling higher, it is the next season
          arriving inside the window we were using to measure the aftermath. So this title is out
          of the table above, and the largest wave in the data tells us nothing about what waves
          leave behind.
        </p>
      ))}
    </section>
  )}

  <section>
    <h2>Fifteen titles we could not measure, and why</h2>
    <p>
      This is most of what we fetched. A question about before-and-after needs a peak with room on
      both sides of it, and most titles do not have that inside a four-year window.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Why not</th><th>Titles</th><th>How many</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {까닭별.map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{g.why}</td>
              <td class="fine">{g.titles.join(', ')}</td>
              <td>{g.titles.length}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  {d.peakClustering && (
    <section>
      <h2>The peaks are not spread out, which is a problem for the question</h2>
      <p>{d.peakClustering.note}</p>
      <p class="note">
        The clusters — {d.peakClustering.clusterMonths.map((c) => c.join(' and ')).join(' · ')}.
        {' '}{d.peakClustering.largestCluster} of the {d.peakClustering.peaks} titles peaked in
        neighbouring months. A title that peaks when another title peaks may be riding something
        those months did to these editions rather than anything of its own. We cannot separate the
        two, and it is the largest reason to treat the table above as a first look rather than an
        answer.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section>
    <h2>What this page cannot tell you</h2>
    {d.cannotSay.map((s) => <p class="note">{s}</p>)}
    {d.droppedLastMonth && (
      <p class="note">
        ⚠ {d.droppedLastMonth.month} is excluded. It arrived at {d.droppedLastMonth.value} against
        a mean of {d.droppedLastMonth.previousTwelveMean} over the twelve months before it —
        Wikimedia's monthly totals were still filling when we fetched, and reading that month as a
        collapse in attention would have been wrong.
      </p>
    )}
    <p class="note">Reads — Wikimedia Pageviews API, human traffic only, monthly.</p>
  </section>

  <KcwCardnews set="wave" count={5} />

  <KcwShorts set="wave"
    says={`A man and a woman read out how many titles could be measured on both sides of their `
      + `peak, and what the floor did afterwards.`} />

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/wave-and-floor" />
</WikiTip>
